What was taken: the lifecycle recipe. Serialize create and cleanup per path;
remove a stale registration and directory before create; retry `git worktree
add` once after clearing stale metadata; verify that the path exists after git
reports success; prune after cleanup; clean orphans only inside an app-owned
base directory.

What changed:
- Every git operation shells out to the git CLI. Upstream validates worktrees
  and creates branches through git2 (libgit2). The sandboxing evidence
  (docs/decisions/evidence/2026-09-24-sandboxing-and-worktrees.md) rules that
  out: git2 worktree validation reports success for a missing directory.
- Worktrees are created locked with a kGraf-tagged reason. Orphan cleanup
  keys on that reason plus owner-process liveness (evidence recipe). Upstream
  does not lock.
- Upstream logs cleanup failures at debug level and continues. Here a failure
  that leaves state behind is returned (SR-008 fail loud). The "not
  registered" case is checked against the porcelain listing, not assumed.
- Upstream keeps a process-global mutex map. Here the per-path guard lives in
  the `WorkspaceManager` value (no hidden globals).
- The API is synchronous. Callers on an async runtime use spawn_blocking.
